{"name":"Glittering Sun","hex":"#d3ad77","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"glittering","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["present participle and gerund of glitter"]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Brightly sparkling.","Valuable, desirable."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/glittering"},{"term":"sun","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-2 & ISO 639-3 language code for Sundanese."]},{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["The star that is closest to the Earth."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sun"}],"entries":[{"title":"Glitter","blocks":[{"text":"Glitter is an assortment of flat, small, reflective particles that are precision cut and come in a variety of shapes, sizes, and colors. Glitter particles resemble confetti, sparkles and sequins, but are somewhat smaller."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Glitter_close_up.jpg/330px-Glitter_close_up.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glitter","wikidata":"Q1531522"},{"title":"Sun","blocks":[{"text":"The Sun is the star located at the centre of the Solar System. It is a massive sphere of hot plasma, heated to incandescence by nuclear fusion reactions in its core, radiating the energy from its surface mainly as visible light and infrared radiation, with 10% at ultraviolet energies. It is the main source of energy for life on Earth. The Sun has been an object of veneration in many cultures and a central subject of astronomical research since antiquity."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/The_Sun_in_white_light.jpg/330px-The_Sun_in_white_light.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sun","wikidata":"Q525"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Mica_3157.jpg/500px-Mica_3157.jpg?utm_source=en.w